# Limitations and open validation gates

## What the current release exactly models

- finite horizons and finite state spaces;
- exact rational action-outcome distributions;
- bounded magic-resource inventories;
- releases, deadlines, injection capacities, and synthetic action costs;
- exact semantic alternatives in a CNOT-plus-dyadic-phase fragment;
- fixed-sample stationary Bernoulli calibration;
- IID product future yields when that assumption is explicitly selected;
- small exact joint/dependence and common-mode countercertificates;
- deterministic recipe-switch latency in a restricted equal-probability theorem.

## What it does not model

### Physical factory dynamics

- multi-cycle pipelines and overlapping in-flight batches;
- outcome observation delay;
- recipe reconfiguration protocols, warm-up, flush, and restart;
- stochastic switch latency;
- state aging, transport loss, storage error, or expiry;
- multi-level distillation/cultivation lineage;
- correlated rejection and input-state quality.

### Architecture and execution

- 2D lattice-surgery patch geometry;
- routing corridors, collisions, merges, splits, and code-distance-dependent timing;
- online latency of the retained ten-CNOT CCZ alternative;
- measurement and feedforward latency;
- Pauli-frame dependencies;
- decoder/controller latency;
- logical-error composition and code-distance selection.

### Statistics

- anytime-valid calibration in the release’s positive provisioning path;
- nonstationary drift or change-point monitoring;
- hardware/configuration identity and certificate expiry;

[Publication excerpt: middle of the long tool response omitted.]

- switching/pipeline costs consume the pooling slack;
- contingent alternatives are highly correlated or share a dominant failure domain;
- online execution latency removes unique deadline-feasible availability;
- real traces show no certified qubit-cycle reduction versus established schedulers;
- calibration changes faster than a certificate can be renewed.

The proof-carrying architecture can remain useful even when a particular optimization benefit is falsified.

